<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">NON-DEGREE STUDENTS</div>
      <p>Anyone who wants to attend classes at UAF as a non-degree student may register as long as they have the appropriate permissions. Non-degree students are not eligible for financial aid.</p>
      <p>Non-degree students are subject to placement examination requirements. Students who are not eligible for placement exams may be required to take placement exams.</p>
      <p>It's important for potential graduate students to understand the requirements for admission to a graduate program. Please see the Graduate School website for more information.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Course Placement</div>
      <div class="sub-section-title">PLACEMENT REQUIREMENTS</div>
      <p>Many UAF courses require placement. All students planning to take courses with specific placement requirements must meet those requirements before registering for those courses. Placement requirements are listed in the course catalog.</p>
      <p>Students need mathematics placement at DEVM F105 or above and ENGL F11X placement for register to core science courses.</p>
      <div class="sub-section-title">PLACEMENT TESTS</div>
      <p>Test results are required for first-time degree or certificate students, transfer students, and students planning to take 100-level English, reading, mathematics, natural sciences, and general education courses. UAF Mathematics placement test results must be on file with the Office of Admissions and the Registrar or the local testing center. UAF English placement test results must be on file with the Office of Admissions and the Registrar. Students who have not submitted required placement test results may be blocked from registering for courses.</p>
      <p>Writing placement exams must be taken within two calendar years before the start of a course; mathematics placement tests must be taken within one calendar year prior to the start of a course. Students enrolling in developmental or lower-division courses must have completed the required placement test.</p>
      <div class="sub-section-title">COURSE PREREQUISITES</div>
      <p>Course prerequisites indicate what previous preparation is needed to enroll in a course. An instructor has the right to deny enrollment in a course if the student does not meet the prerequisite or has not received a grade of C- or better in the prerequisite course. Students must meet all prerequisites for a course to be enrolled in the course. Students who take a course at a higher level than the prerequisite course may be required to take the prerequisite course.</p>
      <div class="sub-section-title">WRITING</div>
      <p>Writing into writing courses requires either prerequisite course credit or a standardized placement test that measures academic skills such as critical thinking and reading. The score from any of the tests listed in the course catalog will be used in the appropriate writing class. A writing sample, given on the first day of class, may modify this placement.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">HIGH SCHOOL AND SECONDARY SCHOOL STUDENTS</div>
      <p>UAF offers programs for high school and secondary school students. These programs are designed to provide opportunities for students to earn college credit while still in high school. Students must meet all requirements for the program they wish to enroll in.</p>
      <p>Secondary student enrollment process allows second-ary school students to register for UAF classes. A student must meet the requirements for the program they wish to enroll in. Students must also meet the requirements for the program they wish to enroll in.</p>
      <p>Registering for courses at UAF establishes a per-sonal academic record that reflects student academic performance in all courses attempted. Students must meet the requirements for the program they wish to enroll in.</p>
      <div class="sub-section-title">TECH PREP OPPORTUNITIES</div>
      <p>The TECH PREP program allows students to earn credits toward a UAF certificate or associate degree by completing courses in high school that have been approved for college credit by UAF. The TECH PREP program is available for students in the following areas: applied business; automotive; airframe and powerplant; computer science; culinary arts; health; drafting; emergency medical services; and welding. For more information, contact your high school counselor or the Community and Technical College at 907-455-2800.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="width: 50%;">
      <div class="section-title">Academic Advising is Required</div>
      <p>Academic advising is an important part of planning for your education. Degree students must obtain an academic advisor's signature every semester to begin the registration process. All degree and certificate students are required to have an academic advisor. Your advisor will work in tandem with your academic advisor to develop a viable educational plan that reflects your academic goals. Your advisor will assist you in determining the best options, alternatives and sequences of classes to take. Non-degree students may also see an academic advisor, and it is recommended for those taking 9 or more credits in a semester or for those who have not yet declared a major.</p>
      <p>Academic advising is available at Services and Resources, page 73, for several campuses.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

